WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:04.809 --> 00:00:09.060
So I know I don't have
a lot of time to talk.

00:00:09.061 --> 00:00:12.359
So let me just sort of get right
smack into the middle of this,

00:00:12.360 --> 00:00:15.531
I was asked to talk about urban
and transportation related

00:00:15.532 --> 00:00:19.340
things, but I will sort of talk
a little bit more broadly.

00:00:19.341 --> 00:00:23.560
We are a think tank based
in Sri Lanka history of,

00:00:23.561 --> 00:00:25.210
we're about 12 years old,

00:00:25.211 --> 00:00:29.400
but very much in the sort of ICT
policy and regulation space.

00:00:29.401 --> 00:00:32.560
But then we started looking at
how can ICTs be used in other

00:00:32.561 --> 00:00:35.320
sectors so
agriculture was one of those.

00:00:35.321 --> 00:00:40.030
And since 2012 we've
been working on big data,

00:00:40.031 --> 00:00:44.540
trying to use that for specific
developmental policy domains.

00:00:44.541 --> 00:00:47.050
But the work is only
in Sri Lanka and

00:00:47.051 --> 00:00:49.010
a little bit in Bangladesh.

00:00:49.011 --> 00:00:53.182
So these are some problems
of interest to us.

00:00:53.183 --> 00:00:57.523
Cities very much, we understand
that given the region that we

00:00:57.524 --> 00:01:02.201
are located in, large amounts of
the population is in the cities.

00:01:02.202 --> 00:01:06.890
And they're growing at a
tremendous rate which is not in

00:01:06.891 --> 00:01:09.200
the most planned fashion.

00:01:09.201 --> 00:01:13.740
So what can we do in that
scenario when there's limited

00:01:13.741 --> 00:01:18.110
planning and limited sense of
data that we can bring to bear.

00:01:18.111 --> 00:01:20.870
So primarily what we're
thinking of is using

00:01:22.420 --> 00:01:25.970
data from mobile operators
as a proxy files,

00:01:25.971 --> 00:01:28.930
as human sensors for
a lot of different things.

00:01:28.931 --> 00:01:32.610
These are the four areas of the
research practice that I lead,

00:01:32.611 --> 00:01:34.120
that it looks at.

00:01:34.121 --> 00:01:37.950
One is conducting
the analytics for answering

00:01:37.951 --> 00:01:41.600
specific problems in different
domains or to develop insights

00:01:41.601 --> 00:01:45.370
that are fed in the specialists
working in these domains.

00:01:45.371 --> 00:01:47.470
Deeply engaging with
the policy makers.

00:01:47.471 --> 00:01:50.598
We actually, in a way,
come from the other side, not so

00:01:50.599 --> 00:01:52.329
much from the analysis side but

00:01:52.330 --> 00:01:55.276
given our historical
linkages with policy makers.

00:01:55.277 --> 00:01:59.646
And working in that space,
trying to work with them and

00:01:59.647 --> 00:02:04.017
the symbolic environment so
not just a policy maker but

00:02:04.018 --> 00:02:08.864
in that specialist the domain
of transportation which can be

00:02:08.865 --> 00:02:10.900
broadly defined.

00:02:10.901 --> 00:02:15.345
Also studying the impacts
of it on society

00:02:15.346 --> 00:02:20.558
privacy marginalization
competition issues.

00:02:20.559 --> 00:02:23.979
So we do have sort of our
founder is a lawyer as well so

00:02:23.980 --> 00:02:28.116
you know that's the area that we
sort of look at quite a bit just

00:02:28.117 --> 00:02:31.936
because it's also an area that
sort of can counter some of

00:02:31.937 --> 00:02:35.358
the work that we are doing
in terms of trying to limit

00:02:35.359 --> 00:02:36.263
the impact.

00:02:36.264 --> 00:02:39.362
And we want to sort of
bring evidence grounded in

00:02:39.363 --> 00:02:43.020
reality into that discourse
on a regional level.

00:02:43.021 --> 00:02:43.570
So that, for

00:02:43.571 --> 00:02:47.940
example, we convened with sort
of regulators in the region.

00:02:47.941 --> 00:02:52.530
We sort of talk to them on a
regular basis, also with ITU and

00:02:52.531 --> 00:02:55.710
the related bodies in
the regional, APT, for example,

00:02:55.711 --> 00:02:59.560
in trying to make access to
telecom data more easier for

00:02:59.561 --> 00:03:05.950
others in the region and
developing guidelines.

00:03:05.951 --> 00:03:08.140
So I'll just give you some
examples of what we are doing

00:03:08.141 --> 00:03:12.010
with CDR data everyone knows
what CDR data is, so this is

00:03:12.011 --> 00:03:16.210
essentially your metadata
every time you use your phone.

00:03:16.211 --> 00:03:19.170
So when you make a call,
receive a call, send an SMS,

00:03:19.171 --> 00:03:24.290
receive an SMS,
initiate an Internet session,

00:03:24.291 --> 00:03:27.730
do a reload,
these get automatically created

00:03:27.731 --> 00:03:32.240
in the network that
the operators have this data.

00:03:32.241 --> 00:03:37.370
And this is metadata, so there's
no actual content and we've

00:03:37.371 --> 00:03:41.510
got basically for about half
the population of Sri Lanka,

00:03:41.511 --> 00:03:45.090
10 million plus people through
multiple years records,

00:03:45.091 --> 00:03:47.050
pseudonymized so
we don't know who they are.

00:03:47.051 --> 00:03:48.250
We don't know demographics,

00:03:48.251 --> 00:03:53.420
we don't know economics, it's
just a mass of people's data.

00:03:53.421 --> 00:03:57.370
So using that for a variety of
how things are trying to get

00:03:57.371 --> 00:04:02.100
more granular and high frequency
estimates of population density.

00:04:02.101 --> 00:04:06.028
Understanding changes in
population density, the dyno

00:04:06.029 --> 00:04:09.953
cycle, circadian rhythms of
people moving into an out of

00:04:09.954 --> 00:04:13.817
cities in terms of these
places that they leave behind.

00:04:13.818 --> 00:04:15.680
And these do have implications,
for

00:04:15.681 --> 00:04:18.130
example, this in
terms of transport.

00:04:18.131 --> 00:04:21.956
What you see here is this is
Colombo City and the picture

00:04:21.957 --> 00:04:27.160
shows the relative density of
a place relative to midnight.

00:04:27.161 --> 00:04:31.811
So what it is like at 6:30,
12:30, 6:30 in the evening.

00:04:31.812 --> 00:04:34.739
And so this is a weekend,
weekday so

00:04:34.740 --> 00:04:39.229
during the weekday noon,
that's when you have sort of

00:04:39.230 --> 00:04:44.126
Colombo City's population
densities highly saturated.

00:04:44.127 --> 00:04:47.475
And the people are generally
coming from the regions outside

00:04:47.476 --> 00:04:48.070
the city.

00:04:48.071 --> 00:04:51.816
So you sort of have a sense of
where people are coming from,

00:04:51.817 --> 00:04:55.485
what the population sort of
hotspots are and you can start

00:04:55.486 --> 00:04:59.099
the thing, use this also as
a way to understand cities.

00:04:59.100 --> 00:05:01.864
There's this thing about
whether cities are monocentric,

00:05:01.865 --> 00:05:04.730
polycentric, what does that
mean for urban planning?

00:05:04.731 --> 00:05:08.090
This is some of that insights
that inform that debate.

00:05:08.091 --> 00:05:11.424
Also when you think about what
should the administrative

00:05:11.425 --> 00:05:14.347
boundary, what should
the limits of the city be?

00:05:14.348 --> 00:05:17.907
So Colombo is actually a very
small city compared to India,

00:05:17.908 --> 00:05:18.911
Indian cities.

00:05:18.912 --> 00:05:23.400
Since we are technically
the population is 550,000 but

00:05:23.401 --> 00:05:26.974
daytime population is
excess of 1.5 million.

00:05:26.975 --> 00:05:29.760
So you can start to think

00:05:29.761 --> 00:05:32.900
the all imposing costs on
the city that aren't recovered,

00:05:32.901 --> 00:05:36.120
from transportation to
garbage collection, etc.

00:05:36.121 --> 00:05:38.149
So what should be the optimal
makeup of cities?

00:05:38.150 --> 00:05:39.260
What should the boundaries be?

00:05:39.261 --> 00:05:42.531
And you can start to play
around with interesting sort of

00:05:42.532 --> 00:05:46.012
possibilities that you can do
with policymakers about what

00:05:46.013 --> 00:05:47.972
should be the makeup
of the city.

00:05:47.973 --> 00:05:50.938
Where you can start to increase
the boundaries to a certain

00:05:50.939 --> 00:05:53.967
manageable population that is
still manageable in terms of

00:05:53.968 --> 00:05:56.040
services that can be
delivered to them.

00:05:57.780 --> 00:06:01.820
So we sort of engage with policy
makers on issues like this.

00:06:02.960 --> 00:06:04.980
On transportation, for example,

00:06:04.981 --> 00:06:09.550
we are sort of trying to think
this is essentially what we

00:06:09.551 --> 00:06:13.160
do is because of all traces
that people leave behind.

00:06:13.161 --> 00:06:18.370
We can construct the network
of mobility and we are doing

00:06:18.371 --> 00:06:22.560
modularity optimization to set
of do some partitioning of this.

00:06:22.561 --> 00:06:27.483
And so what essentially this
means is that each colored

00:06:27.484 --> 00:06:32.405
region is a region where
the density of travel within it

00:06:32.406 --> 00:06:34.773
is a greater than across.

00:06:34.774 --> 00:06:37.153
And you can think of this
being plugged into last-mile

00:06:37.154 --> 00:06:38.091
solutions, right?

00:06:38.092 --> 00:06:41.143
If you have the bus that's
coming from point a to point b,

00:06:41.144 --> 00:06:44.260
this would be where the three
wheel associations you might

00:06:44.261 --> 00:06:45.036
want to have.

00:06:45.037 --> 00:06:48.880
You can think more broadly and
think about sort of

00:06:48.881 --> 00:06:53.196
localized transport schemes for
park and ride options

00:06:53.197 --> 00:06:57.340
based on where people start and
where people end up.

00:06:57.341 --> 00:06:58.650
We are also, for example,

00:06:58.651 --> 00:07:00.820
starting to impacts of
new infrastructure,

00:07:00.821 --> 00:07:04.640
this is work that I do with
Joshua Blumenstock from

00:07:04.641 --> 00:07:07.970
University of California,
UC Berkeley.

00:07:07.971 --> 00:07:11.360
Where we are looking at
developing new measures of

00:07:11.361 --> 00:07:15.460
trying to understand the impact
of new infrastructure

00:07:15.461 --> 00:07:19.050
on mobility and by extension
understanding the welfare

00:07:19.051 --> 00:07:21.210
impacts on
the population over time.

00:07:21.211 --> 00:07:24.428
Because if you can have this
data, you can understand its

00:07:24.429 --> 00:07:27.123
short term, medium term,
long term impacts.

00:07:30.968 --> 00:07:33.935
This is something that
we're doing right now,

00:07:33.936 --> 00:07:36.170
the field work just
got completed.

00:07:37.390 --> 00:07:39.310
We don't have a lot
of intelligence about

00:07:39.311 --> 00:07:43.430
this 10 million plus people,
we don't know gender,

00:07:43.431 --> 00:07:47.810
we don't know economics,
we don't know demographics.

00:07:47.811 --> 00:07:50.958
So what we are trying to do is
an ideal scenario we would work

00:07:50.959 --> 00:07:53.917
with operators to do phone
service through which we can

00:07:53.918 --> 00:07:55.671
build a good training dataset.

00:07:55.672 --> 00:07:59.044
But that's a little
difficult to accomplish so

00:07:59.045 --> 00:08:02.753
we're doing a slightly next
best solution is sort of

00:08:02.754 --> 00:08:06.969
a parallel activity when we've
used a purposive sample and

00:08:06.970 --> 00:08:10.305
installed farm people
with mobile phones.

00:08:10.306 --> 00:08:14.419
Make sure they are given enough
coverage of gender, age, and

00:08:14.420 --> 00:08:18.600
economics, socioeconomics
level that they are in.

00:08:19.620 --> 00:08:24.734
And have an app that captures
the cell phone behavior as

00:08:24.735 --> 00:08:29.735
well as we do a survey on top,
a sort of the recapturing

00:08:29.736 --> 00:08:33.422
GPS logs as well from
the smartphones.

00:08:33.423 --> 00:08:36.242
But in order to build
understanding for

00:08:36.243 --> 00:08:41.040
transportation policy, we are
asking questions every so often.

00:08:41.041 --> 00:08:45.829
So between the time of 6 AM
to 9 PM, we are asking every

00:08:45.830 --> 00:08:49.891
hour on the hour questions
like where are you,

00:08:49.892 --> 00:08:55.096
what are you doing, if you are
going where are you going to,

00:08:55.097 --> 00:08:58.890
and what form of
transport you're using.

00:08:58.891 --> 00:09:02.120
Through which we can sort of,
we'll basically leverage this as

00:09:02.121 --> 00:09:06.560
a training data to understand
the most of data that we have

00:09:06.561 --> 00:09:09.470
on the other side with
the cell phone records.

00:09:09.471 --> 00:09:13.455
But again, this data is sparse,
is not great for

00:09:13.456 --> 00:09:15.810
understanding traffic
conditions.

00:09:15.811 --> 00:09:18.615
There's VR data which is
good but doesn't kept for

00:09:18.616 --> 00:09:20.355
very long in the networks.

00:09:20.356 --> 00:09:24.625
It doesn't give you very fine
temporal resolution and so

00:09:24.626 --> 00:09:27.395
we are trying to develop
algorithms to do CCTV

00:09:27.396 --> 00:09:29.955
footage working with
the police department there.

00:09:31.980 --> 00:09:35.100
Also trying to understand,
for example, based on

00:09:35.101 --> 00:09:38.450
the circadian rhythms of people
connected to base station,

00:09:38.451 --> 00:09:44.110
can we again identify regions
of high economic activity and

00:09:44.111 --> 00:09:46.980
sort of understand whether
on the highly residential

00:09:46.981 --> 00:09:49.660
to highly commercial
spectrum of areas.

00:09:51.250 --> 00:09:55.010
But that is not good enough, so
we are also looking at satellite

00:09:55.011 --> 00:09:59.190
imagery to understand urban
space a lot more that small.

00:09:59.191 --> 00:10:03.820
The idea being that you can
make all these grand plans and

00:10:03.821 --> 00:10:06.710
visions of what ICT should
be in 20, 30 years but when

00:10:06.711 --> 00:10:08.810
you don't know what's actually
happening on the ground,

00:10:08.811 --> 00:10:09.790
it's kind of pointless.

00:10:12.510 --> 00:10:15.020
But we can also
leverage mobility,

00:10:15.021 --> 00:10:20.090
this is basically using origin
constrained gravity model

00:10:20.091 --> 00:10:24.980
to develop new and proxy
indicators of economic activity.

00:10:24.981 --> 00:10:28.753
These track very well with night
time lights, but they have

00:10:28.754 --> 00:10:32.753
the added advantage that this
is a finer temporal resolution,

00:10:32.754 --> 00:10:35.259
you can think about
doing this weekly.

00:10:35.260 --> 00:10:38.356
I mean, this is a great measure
of understanding on a very

00:10:38.357 --> 00:10:41.392
localized basis so like
yesterday we were talking about

00:10:41.393 --> 00:10:43.071
regions at a district level but

00:10:43.072 --> 00:10:46.059
here you can potentially
go down, even further down.

00:10:46.060 --> 00:10:49.388
So this is work in progress
that we're doing, and

00:10:49.389 --> 00:10:52.970
we're feeding into the sort
of a visual statistics.

00:10:54.950 --> 00:10:57.960
These are sort of broad
implications of this work

00:10:57.961 --> 00:11:02.720
that we're working
with different domains.

00:11:02.721 --> 00:11:06.936
So open and transportation
policy, epidemiology unit on

00:11:06.937 --> 00:11:09.779
understanding disease
propagation.

00:11:12.391 --> 00:11:13.078
Yeah.

00:11:13.079 --> 00:11:16.677
So that's in a brief, the kind
of work that we're doing,

00:11:16.678 --> 00:11:17.415
thank you.

00:11:17.416 --> 00:11:23.804
&gt;&gt; [APPLAUSE]
&gt;&gt; A quick question

00:11:23.805 --> 00:11:27.599
how closely are you working with
the government, does it matter,

00:11:27.600 --> 00:11:30.970
these things in the sense
of policy recommendations?

00:11:30.971 --> 00:11:34.050
&gt;&gt; So when we started this there
was absolutely no demand, we

00:11:34.051 --> 00:11:37.285
sort of were under the radar by
purpose because we didn't know,

00:11:37.286 --> 00:11:40.020
we needed to be said we had to
figure out all the implications.

00:11:40.021 --> 00:11:41.790
So we didn't talk about it for
a good year and

00:11:41.791 --> 00:11:44.900
a half and it took about
a year to negotiate the data.

00:11:44.901 --> 00:11:48.860
So initially I would advise as I
said to some of you I was like

00:11:48.861 --> 00:11:50.505
a Jehovah's Witness
going door to door,

00:11:50.506 --> 00:11:52.945
have you heard
the word of big data?

00:11:52.946 --> 00:11:57.475
But now it's more like
they will give us calls,

00:11:57.476 --> 00:11:59.045
we are doing x, y and z.

00:11:59.046 --> 00:12:01.755
So there was a huge amount of
policy enlightenment work in

00:12:01.756 --> 00:12:05.725
the beginning doing workshops,
doing meetings

00:12:05.726 --> 00:12:09.810
to tell them the different ways
that you can use this for.

00:12:09.811 --> 00:12:12.600
So now it's at different
levels so it's not always that

00:12:12.601 --> 00:12:15.150
the senior person knows,
a policy maker knows all of

00:12:15.151 --> 00:12:17.350
the interactions that their
department is having with us.

00:12:18.450 --> 00:12:23.100
So large amount but the help,
for example, is more tricky.

00:12:23.101 --> 00:12:24.480
We are still working with them,

00:12:24.481 --> 00:12:26.420
we are working with
the police department.

00:12:28.760 --> 00:12:32.790
But that's a long
engagement of having for

00:12:32.791 --> 00:12:35.271
long periods of time, yeah.

00:12:35.272 --> 00:12:37.296
&gt;&gt; Great, thank you.

00:12:37.297 --> 00:12:40.577
&gt;&gt; [APPLAUSE]

